<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Query limits for the default user profile.

  Set server-side rather than per-query because three different clients issue heavy queries
  against this database — dbt, the FastAPI query compiler, and whoever is at a SQL prompt —
  and a limit that only one of them sets is a limit that does not exist. Getting this wrong
  is not a slow query, it is MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED and a failed build.

  **Sized against the container's 4G default cap in docker-compose.yml. Keep the two in step.**
  The ceiling must stay BELOW the container limit: if a query is allowed more memory than the
  container has, Docker OOM-kills the whole server instead of ClickHouse rejecting one query.
  That turns a failed model into a failed stack.

  These numbers are for the INCREMENTAL workload, which is now the normal one (ADR-019).
  Measured peaks: 591 MiB for a hero session upload, 2.10 GiB for a 5.9M-row pool month, and
  3.17 GiB for the worst single partition (2025-01, 20.5M rows). A stat query over the 54M-row
  fact table uses 13–29 MiB. A full refresh is a different workload — it peaked at 7.30 GiB —
  and is a deliberate two-knob operation:

      CLICKHOUSE_MEM=15G docker compose up -d clickhouse
      DBT_CH_MAX_MEMORY=12000000000 .venv-dbt/bin/dbt build (with the full refresh flag)

  DBT_CH_MAX_MEMORY raises the ceiling for dbt's sessions only (see dbt/poker_dwh/profiles.yml),
  so ad-hoc queries stay bounded by the values below even during a rebuild.

  **This profile is the SERVER-WIDE floor, not the per-tenant budget.** It bounds every client,
  including dbt and the migration runner, and it is the ceiling a tenant's own budget must stay
  under. The per-tenant settings profiles and quotas are created at runtime, from
  stats/budget.py, because a tenant is not a thing a static file can enumerate (plan E.3,
  ADR-043). Four files now have to stay in step, not three: the container cap in
  docker-compose.yml, the caches in small-node.xml, this ceiling, and the numbers in
  stats/budget.py, which must all be lower than this one.

  NOTE: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen. Spell command flags out in prose here
  rather than pasting them literally, or the server refuses to start with a SAXParseException.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <!-- Ceiling for one query: 2.5 GB, which is ~58% of the 4 GiB container cap above.
                 Must stay well below that cap so a runaway query is rejected cleanly instead of
                 Docker OOM-killing the server. Verified: the worst partition rebuild peaks at
                 3.17 GiB, so this leaves real headroom rather than sitting on the edge.

                 The comment here used to read "~69% of the 8G container", which was true of a
                 5.5 GB ceiling in an 8 GB container and has been wrong since both shrank; the
                 figure was carried into docs/POKER_SCALE.md (E.4) as a known doc-drift item and
                 is corrected here in E.3. dbt/poker_dwh/profiles.yml repeats the same stale
                 sentence and is NOT fixed, because that file belongs to the dbt project. -->
            <max_memory_usage>2500000000</max_memory_usage>

            <!-- Spill to disk instead of failing. Held at half the query ceiling so the
                 switch to the external algorithm happens while there is still room to do it. -->
            <max_bytes_before_external_group_by>900000000</max_bytes_before_external_group_by>
            <max_bytes_before_external_sort>900000000</max_bytes_before_external_sort>

            <!-- ClickHouse builds the RIGHT side of a join in memory. The incremental models
                 scope every join side to the dirty partitions, so the right side is now small
                 — but keep the memory-bounded fallback chain for the full-refresh path and for
                 any single month large enough to matter (202501 alone is 20.5M player rows).
                 Order matters: the first algorithm that supports the join's shape wins. -->
            <join_algorithm>grace_hash,partial_merge,hash</join_algorithm>
            <max_bytes_in_join>400000000</max_bytes_in_join>

            <!-- Number of grace-hash buckets. More buckets = smaller working set per pass. -->
            <grace_hash_join_initial_buckets>16</grace_hash_join_initial_buckets>

            <!-- **The biggest single memory lever on a small node.** ClickHouse defaults to one
                 pipeline per core (16 on this laptop) and EACH holds its own join and aggregate
                 state, so peak memory scales with core count, not just with data. The 8-way
                 join in int_hand_player_flags needed 2.56 GiB at 16 threads, 2.27 GiB at 4, and fits
                 the budget at 2.
                 2 is also honest about the target: a cheap production node has 2-4 vCPU, so
                 pinning this here means the laptop measures what production will actually do
                 instead of flattering it with 16 cores. Queries get slower per-query and the
                 box stops falling over, which is the correct trade for a batch rebuild. -->
            <max_threads>2</max_threads>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
